COURSES=[{
'slug':'lean-foundations','title':'Lean 4 for graph engineers.','intro':'A short path from a typed domain model to a value that carries evidence. Three lessons, three exercises, and a precise view of what is being checked.',
'note':'Prerequisites: basic programming. The snippets are small Lean language examples, not an implementation of a graph database. Use the official Lean books and project toolchains for hands-on work.',
'lessons':[
('Model the entities before the edges.','''<p>A graph becomes easier to reason about when its vocabulary has explicit types. A person and an organization may both have names, but they are different kinds of value. A relation can record that difference.</p><pre><code>structure Person where
  name : String

structure Organization where
  name : String

structure Employment where
  person : Person
  organization : Organization</code></pre><p>The fields of <code>Employment</code> specify its endpoints. Swapping a person for an organization does not match this structure’s type. In a real database design you would usually store stable identifiers or typed references rather than duplicate entire values.</p><p>This model says nothing yet about when someone was employed or whether the source is reliable. Types expose the structure of your claim; the next step is choosing its obligations.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>You want to represent membership in an organization. Which parts are ordinary data, and which might require a rule?</p><details class="solution"><summary>Show a worked answer</summary><p>The member and organization are typed data. Start and end dates are data too. A requirement that the end not precede the start is a proposition about those values. Source reliability needs a separate provenance policy; the relation’s shape cannot establish it.</p></details>'''),
('Let a type depend on a value.','''<p>Dependent type theory lets the type of one field refer to another field’s value. That is useful when a valid object must carry evidence of a property of its own data.</p><pre><code>structure BoundedCount (limit : Nat) where
  count : Nat
  withinLimit : count ≤ limit

example : BoundedCount 3 :=
  { count := 2, withinLimit := by decide }</code></pre><p><code>BoundedCount 3</code> is a type. Its proof field refers to the particular count and limit. The example provides both the number and a proof that it satisfies the bound.</p><p>For an ontology, the corresponding idea is a store bundled with a proof of a predicate such as <code>Valid store</code>. You must define that predicate, establish an initial valid store, and prove that each permitted update preserves it.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Does putting a field named <code>valid : Bool</code> into a record provide the same guarantee?</p><details class="solution"><summary>Show a worked answer</summary><p>No. A Boolean field can be set independently of the data. A proof field has a proposition as its type and must supply a term of that proposition. A Boolean checker can still be useful, but you need a soundness theorem connecting its successful result to the desired property.</p></details>'''),
('Make the claim and its assumptions visible.','''<p>In Lean, propositions are types and proofs are terms. A simple theorem can package a piece of evidence supplied by an assumption.</p><pre><code>example (P Q : Prop) (h : P ∧ Q) : P := by
  exact h.left</code></pre><p>This proves that <code>P</code> follows from <code>P ∧ Q</code>. It does not establish either proposition without that assumption. The same discipline applies to a graph: a derivation explains what follows from the input and the rules.</p><p>Imagine two input claims: a cat is a mammal, and Tom is a cat. A formal rule may justify the conclusion that Tom is a mammal. The proof checks the inference. The source and accuracy of the two input claims remain a separate concern.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Your agent proves an answer from yesterday’s graph. Is that enough to answer a question about the current graph?</p><details class="solution"><summary>Show a worked answer</summary><p>Only if the claim’s contract makes that relationship explicit. Bind the certificate to the exact graph snapshot, ontology, and query it concerns. If data changes, recheck or prove that the relevant result is preserved. A proof of one statement is not automatically a proof of a changed statement.</p></details><p>For larger examples, inspect dependencies with Lean’s <code>#print axioms</code> command, avoid unfinished <code>sorry</code> proofs, and understand any native computation used in the checking path.</p>''')],
'next':'<p><a href="https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/">Read Theorem Proving in Lean 4 ↗</a></p><p><a href="/articles/dependent-types/">Read: When your knowledge carries its own proof →</a></p>'
},{
'slug':'elpi-reasoning','title':'ELPI & higher-order reasoning.','intro':'Learn what a logic program searches for, why binders matter, and how a proposed derivation can become checked evidence.',
'note':'Prerequisites: basic programming and the idea of a proposition. ELPI and LeanElpi are related implementations; use the LeanElpi book for the exact syntax and supported behavior in this ecosystem.',
'lessons':[
('Describe a relation. Ask for a witness.','''<p>A logic program states facts and rules. A query asks the engine to find substitutions that make a goal follow. The program describes a relation rather than prescribing every step of an algorithm.</p><pre><code>% A tiny λProlog relation
kind animal type.
type tom animal.
pred cat o:animal.
pred mammal o:animal.
cat tom.
mammal X :- cat X.</code></pre><p>With the query <code>mammal X</code>, the engine can discover <code>X = tom</code>. This is a successful search relative to this program. The declaration and clause spelling should be run with the project’s documented command-line or Lean integration.</p><p>Search and verification are different responsibilities. A printed answer is not, by itself, a Lean theorem. If you need certification, select a path that records derivations and connects them to a checker.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>If the search does not find another mammal, have you proved that none exists?</p><details class="solution"><summary>Show a worked answer</summary><p>No. Search may be incomplete, bounded, or missing facts. Failure to derive a claim is not generally a proof of its negation. Closed-world conclusions need an explicit semantics and the relevant completeness conditions.</p></details>'''),
('Keep binders and assumptions in scope.','''<p>Higher-order representations can make functions and predicates part of the data being manipulated. For syntax containing bound variables, higher-order abstract syntax uses the logic language’s binding machinery to represent object-language binding.</p><pre><code>% A typing rule for an object-language lambda
of (lam F) (arr A B) :-
  pi x\\ of x A => of (F x) B.</code></pre><p>This fragment comes from the kind of simply typed lambda-calculus example developed in LeanElpi’s introduction. Read it as follows: introduce a fresh <code>x</code>; assume locally that <code>x</code> has type <code>A</code>; under that assumption, show that <code>F x</code> has type <code>B</code>.</p><p>The <code>pi</code> binder and hypothetical implication <code>=&gt;</code> make scope explicit. The temporary assumption is used inside the goal. It is not a permanent write to an agent’s database.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Why should a hypothetical question not silently update the shared knowledge graph?</p><details class="solution"><summary>Show a worked answer</summary><p>A conclusion derived under an assumption is conditional on that assumption. Persisting it as an unconditional fact loses the dependency. Keep hypothetical contexts scoped, or store the assumption and provenance as part of the claim’s meaning.</p></details>'''),
('Turn search into a candidate certificate.','''<p>A useful architecture gives the search component freedom to change while keeping the acceptance rule small and explicit.</p><ol><li><strong>State the goal.</strong> Identify the graph snapshot, formal vocabulary, and exact query.</li><li><strong>Search.</strong> Let Elpi construct a derivation using admissible rules.</li><li><strong>Record evidence.</strong> Export a proof object or certificate with the dependencies needed to replay the reasoning.</li><li><strong>Check.</strong> Verify each step and connect successful checking to the goal through a soundness theorem.</li></ol><p>LeanElpi’s optional LF layer and LeanRDF’s certificate checker are two concrete parts of this ecosystem. They have different formats and interfaces; choose the mechanism appropriate to your claim.</p><p>Be precise about execution. A verified checker has a theorem about its behavior. A compiled program running that checker also depends on runtime machinery. Constructing a Lean theorem using native computation has its own trust assumptions. Inspect the actual route used by the project rather than treating all three as interchangeable.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>A certificate is syntactically valid but references a premise absent from the input graph. Should it be accepted?</p><details class="solution"><summary>Show a worked answer</summary><p>No. Parsing only establishes that the object has the expected shape. The checker must validate premises, rule applications, and the link to the requested goal. Missing premises must cause rejection unless they are legitimate assumptions explicitly declared by the contract.</p></details>''')],
'next':'<p><a href="https://volland.github.io/lean-elpi/book/">Open the LeanElpi book ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://volland.github.io/rdf-lean/examples/">Run a LeanRDF example ↗</a></p><p><a href="/articles/higher-order-logic/">Read: Let logic search. Let Lean check. →</a></p>'
}]
